    "lawSection": "Homeland Security",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1743\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act in relation to enacting the \"non-profit homeland security\npreparedness study\"; and providing for the repeal of such provisions\nupon expiration thereof\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF THE BILL :\nThis bill would study the need for, and feasibility of, establishing a\nstate program to parallel the existing inadequate and sporadic federal\nprogram to assist nonprofits with the burdens of enhancing their\nsecurity from threats of terrorism, which inexplicably didn't function\nat all in 2006 and which in 2007 awarded non-profit institutions in\nNew York a measly $3.3 million in grants to 43 institutions (fewer\nthan half of those who applied for grants).\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection one describes the impact of increased post-9/l1 homeland\nsecurity preparedness on the state's nonprofit and cultural\ninstitutions, as well as the federal government's inadequate\nassistance to institutions. Section two directs OHS to study, evaluate\nand make recommendations in writing to the Governor, the temporary\npresident of the Senate, and the speaker of the assembly about the\nuniverse of state resources available to assist non-profit\ninstitutions in hardening their facilities through a state non-profit\ngrant program, create criteria of eligibility of such program, and\ncreate criteria to conduct risk or vulnerability assessment on\nnon-profit organizations throughout the state. Section three provides\nthat OHS shall complete the study by December 31, 2008.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nNew York State is home to hundreds, if not thousands, of non-profit\ncultural, religious and educational institutions which are terrorist\ntargets. These institutions, which selflessly serve the needs of\nmillions of New Yorkers, have to expend precious resources to enhance\ntheir security in order to protect against this terrorist threat. Many\ninstitutions cannot afford to make these vital improvements at all for\nlack of funds, leaving the institutions themselves and the people they\nserve vulnerable to attack. It is fundamentally unfair for these\ninstitutions to shoulder the entire burden of defending themselves\nagainst the increased threat of terrorism when all of us as New\nYorkers benefit from the services they provide. The existing federal\nprogram is both inadequate in the amount of money it provides and\nunreliable in when and how it functions. New York State institutions\nreceived barely $3.3 million in 2007, and less than half of the 109\nNew York institutions who applied got any funding support at all. The\nfederal program mysteriously disappeared entirely in 2006 and there is\nno guarantee that it will continue in 2008. New York must step up to\nthe plate to offer assistance to the non-profit institutions who\ncontribute so much to our state, with a program under our guidance and\ncontrol, and responsive to our needs.  This legislation lays the\ngroundwork for establishing such a program.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew Bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
